<script>
     const COUNTER_CONFIG = {
            duration: 1000,
            steps: 60,
            threshold: 0.5
        };

        function initializeCounters(sectionClass, itemClass, valueSelector) {
            const { duration, steps, threshold } = COUNTER_CONFIG;

            function animateCounter(element, targetValue, originalText) {
                let currentValue = 0;
                const increment = targetValue / steps;
                const stepTime = duration / steps;
                
                const symbols = originalText.replace(/[\d.]/g, '');

                const counter = setInterval(() => {
                    currentValue += increment;

                    if (Number.isInteger(targetValue)) {
                        element.textContent = Math.round(currentValue) + symbols;
                    } else {
                        element.textContent = currentValue.toFixed(3) + symbols;
                    }

                    if (currentValue >= targetValue) {
                        element.textContent = originalText;
                        clearInterval(counter);
                    }
                }, stepTime);
            }

            const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
                entries.forEach(entry => {
                    if (entry.isIntersecting) {
                        const valueElement = entry.target.querySelector(valueSelector);
                        // const originalText = valueElement.textContent;
                        const originalText = valueElement.textContent.trim();
                        if (!/\d/.test(originalText)) {
                            observer.unobserve(entry.target);
                            return;
                        }

                        const targetValue = parseFloat(originalText.replace(/[^\d.]/g, ''));
                        valueElement.textContent = '0' + originalText.replace(/[\d.]/g, '');
                        animateCounter(valueElement, targetValue, originalText);
                        observer.unobserve(entry.target);
                    }
                });
            }, { threshold });

            document.querySelectorAll(sectionClass).forEach(section => {
                section.querySelectorAll(itemClass).forEach(item => {
                    observer.observe(item);
                });
            });
        }

        initializeCounters('.home-banner', '.stat', 'strong');
</script>

<section class="content-sec">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="content-cont">
            <div class="content">
                <h2>Build the Right <strong>Solution Before You</strong> Commit to Production</h2><p>A strong custom box is not created by choosing isolated options from a list. Product fit, channel, structure, material, printing, finishes, interior arrangement, quantity and timing must work as one plan. The following decisions help turn a broad idea into a production-ready solution without losing the customer outcome that made customization necessary in the first place.</p><ol>
                <li>
                    <div class="image-box">
                        <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/details-content-icon_1785233336.svg" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="details content icon 1785233336" width="40" height="38" class="lazy">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <strong>	Define the Business Job Before the Box Specification	</strong>
                        <p>
	Start with the moment the box must improve. Is the product competing at retail, arriving through delivery, being handed over as a gift, presenting a multi-item kit, supporting a launch or organizing a full range? Decide what the customer should understand first: product type, quality level, brand identity, occasion, variant, contents or opening order. This direction prevents the project from becoming a collection of expensive but disconnected choices. A rigid structure may add substance, but it should only be used when that presence fits the product and customer moment. An interior print may extend the brand, but only where opening matters. A window may clarify the product, but only where visibility supports selection. The team can then recommend a route around the result rather than forcing the brief into a familiar template.	</p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <div class="image-box">
                        <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/details-content-icon_1785233336.svg" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="details content icon 1785233336" width="40" height="38" class="lazy">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <strong>	Size the Box Around the Real Packed Product	</strong>
                        <p>
	Provide the product’s length, width and height, then include everything that changes the packed dimensions: inner containers, wrapping, accessories, grouped units, trays, dividers, foam, protective layers or presentation spacing. For irregular products, share the shape, orientation and any surfaces that need special consideration. The objective is not simply to make the product fit. The structure should allow practical packing, controlled movement where relevant and natural removal. No clearance, size range or pack count should be invented without an approved specification. For sets and kits, confirm the number of components, their arrangement and the order in which customers should see or remove them. This gives the dieline a real configuration to solve.	</p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <div class="image-box">
                        <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/details-content-icon_1785233336.svg" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="details content icon 1785233336" width="40" height="38" class="lazy">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <strong>	Select the Structure From the Channel and Customer Moment	</strong>
                        <p>
	Straight tuck, reverse tuck, auto-bottom, lock-bottom, sleeve, two-piece, window, gable, pillow, pyramid, display, mailer, custom-shape and rigid formats are available, subject to the approved specification. Inserts, dividers, trays, handles, hang tabs, magnetic closures and ribbon pulls can be added where they serve the structure and use. The choice should follow the channel. A retail carton needs clear comparison and usable information panels. A mailer needs delivery suitability and a receiving sequence. A rigid gift box needs physical presence and a deliberate handoff. A counter display needs organized visibility. A launch kit may need several items, messages and layers to work together. Round and tube boxes are not offered. Large commodity shipping cartons are not the primary focus, although corrugated board can support relevant product, mailer, promotional, presentation and kit applications.	</p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <div class="image-box">
                        <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/details-content-icon_1785233336.svg" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="details content icon 1785233336" width="40" height="38" class="lazy">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <strong>	Connect Material, Print, Finish and Interior as One System	</strong>
                        <p>
	Paperboard, cardstock and kraft support a wide range of retail, product, gift and custom-shape boxes. Rigid greyboard adds substance to selected gifts, sets and presentation formats. Corrugated board is available in E, B, F and micro-flute routes for relevant structured applications. Paperboard specifications from 14 pt to 24 pt are available where appropriate to the approved design. Digital, offset and flexographic printing are available, together with CMYK, PMS, metallic ink, full-color coverage and white ink on kraft. Printing can be planned across outside and relevant inside surfaces. Matte, gloss and soft-touch laminations, spot UV, foil, embossing, debossing, aqueous coating and raised effects can then shape attention and surface feel. The team should connect these choices to one intended perception. A smooth paperboard box with controlled color may communicate precision. Kraft with white ink may preserve material character while keeping the brand clear. A rigid box with a wrapped surface and fitted interior may support a more substantial gift or set experience. The system matters more than any single option.	</p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <div class="image-box">
                        <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/details-content-icon_1785233336.svg" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="details content icon 1785233336" width="40" height="38" class="lazy">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <strong>	Prepare Artwork and Approval Inputs Before Production	</strong>
                        <p>
	AI, PDF, PSD and EPS files are accepted. Dieline or template support, artwork checking, resizing, bleed and safe-zone assistance and design support are available. No die or plate fee may be stated where the approved production route supports it. Artwork should be placed on the approved structure so folds, cut lines, windows, closures and inside panels can be reviewed in context. A digital proof is available for every approved order. It allows the approved design and production layout to be checked before printing, but it is not a physical prototype. Production does not begin before payment, final artwork approval and structure confirmation. The buyer remains responsible for supplying and approving any legally required or regulated product information.	</p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <div class="image-box">
                        <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/details-content-icon_1785233336.svg" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="details content icon 1785233336" width="40" height="38" class="lazy">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <strong>	Use Quantity Planning to Protect Flexibility and Unit Cost	</strong>
                        <p>
	Paperboard, cardstock, kraft and supported corrugated orders may start from 50 boxes. Supported rigid-box orders may start from 25 boxes, including relevant rigid formats with foam inserts. A low starting quantity does not automatically remove relevant customization choices; sizing, print coverage, finishes and inserts remain available where they suit the approved structure and production specification. Per-unit pricing generally improves as quantity rises. More visible price differences may appear around 500 units, but no fixed saving percentage should be assumed. The quote will reflect the box style, material, print, finish, insert, quantity, design count and production setup. One-box production is not offered. A physical prototype may be available for a qualifying bulk or wholesale project, and its cost may be adjusted into the resulting order where approved. This is different from an unrestricted free-sample offer.	</p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <div class="image-box">
                        <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/details-content-icon_1785233336.svg" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="details content icon 1785233336" width="40" height="38" class="lazy">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <strong>	Confirm the Production, Design and Delivery Plan	</strong>
                        <p>
	Production normally takes 4–6 business days after payment, final artwork approval and structure confirmation. Production-ready jobs are generally planned to print and ship within 7 business days. Shipping transit time is separate, while complex multi-design orders may require a separately confirmed schedule. Share the number of designs, quantity per design, delivery addresses, packing instructions and target date before approval. One order can be shipped to multiple addresses, and workflows are available for design firms and white-label projects. Several designs at low quantities may create additional setups, so the schedule should reflect the actual program rather than a single-design assumption. A useful quote request includes product dimensions, packed configuration, intended channel, preferred structure, material direction, outside and inside print needs, finishes, inserts, quantity, design count, destinations and timing. Clear inputs allow the team to solve the brief directly and keep the approved customer experience connected to production	</p>
                    </div>
                </li></ol>            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faqs">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="content">
            <p><strong>	CUSTOM BOX DECISIONS	</strong></p><h2>Clear <strong>Answers Before</strong> You Build and Order</h2>        </div>
        <div class="wrapper">
            <div class="accordions custom_scroll">
                                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> Can you create a custom box when I only have the product and an idea?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>Yes. You can begin with the product, its dimensions, the intended sales or delivery moment and the impression the box should create. The team can help turn those inputs into a suitable structure, dieline, material, print plan, finish direction and interior arrangement. Design support, artwork checking, resizing, bleed and safe-zone assistance are available. The goal is to give the brief a workable packaging route, not to make you choose technical options before the product and business need are understood.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> How broad is the range of custom box solutions available?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>The range covers retail cartons, sleeves, two-piece boxes, mailers, rigid boxes, gift packaging, displays, kits, window boxes, handled formats, custom shapes and insert-led solutions, subject to the approved production specification. Those routes can support individual products, collections, subscriptions, PR packages, launches, events, corporate gifts and multi-component sets. The important advantage is not variety by itself. It is the ability to combine a suitable structure, material, print, finish and interior around the actual customer moment.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> Can one box combine custom structure, printing, finishes and inserts?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>Yes. A single approved solution can coordinate sizing, structure, outside and inside printing, laminations, foil, spot UV, embossing, debossing, raised effects, windows, handles, closures, inserts, dividers or trays where they suit the design. The team plans those elements around one purpose, such as clearer retail recognition, a more substantial gift handoff, organized kit discovery or a connected delivery experience. Options are not added automatically; each one should earn its place by improving how the package looks, feels, functions or opens.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> Can you keep several products connected without making every box identical?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>Yes. A range can share a parent-brand system while giving each product, flavor, scent, shade, model or edition its own identity. Printing can coordinate color logic, typography, imagery, information order and panel use across multiple designs. Structure and material can remain consistent where that supports production and recognition, or vary where different products need different formats. Multi-design orders are supported, although projects with many short runs or setups may require a separately confirmed production schedule.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> Do low starting quantities reduce my customization choices?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>No—not automatically. Paperboard, cardstock, kraft and supported corrugated boxes may start from 50 units, while supported rigid formats may start from 25. Relevant custom sizing, outside or inside printing, finishes and inserts remain available where they suit the approved structure and production specification. This allows a growing business to test a launch, introduce a new range or prepare a focused promotion without being pushed toward generic stock packaging or an unnecessarily large first order.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> What box styles and shapes can you produce?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>A broad range is available, including straight tuck, reverse tuck, auto-bottom, lock-bottom, sleeve, two-piece, window, gable, pillow, pyramid, display, mailer, custom-shape and rigid structures. Handles, hang tabs, magnetic closures, ribbon pulls, inserts, dividers and trays can be added where relevant. Round and tube boxes are not offered. Large commodity shipping cartons are not the main focus, although corrugated material can be used for relevant product, mailer, promotional, presentation and kit-box applications.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> Can I print on both the outside and inside of the box?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>Yes. Printing can be planned across the front, back, top, bottom, sides and relevant inside surfaces where the approved structure allows. Digital, offset and flexographic production are available, together with CMYK, PMS, full-color, metallic ink and white ink on kraft. The team can help decide which surfaces should carry recognition, product information, campaign messaging or opening cues so the print coverage supports the customer journey instead of repeating artwork without purpose.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> What information should I send for accurate sizing and quoting?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>Send the product’s length, width and height, then include anything that changes the packed size: inner containers, wrapping, accessories, grouped units, inserts, dividers, trays or foam. Also share the intended box use, preferred structure or opening style, material direction, outside and inside print needs, finishes, quantity, number of designs, delivery destinations and target date. For sets or irregular products, explain the component arrangement and how items should be removed. Exact clearances or structural dimensions should be confirmed through the approved dieline rather than assumed.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> What artwork and proofing support do I receive before printing?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>You receive practical preparation support and a digital proof for every approved order. Accepted files include AI, PDF, PSD and EPS. Dieline or template help, artwork checking, resizing, bleed and safe-zone assistance and design support are available. The digital proof shows the approved artwork on the production layout so the design can be checked before printing; it is not a physical prototype. Production starts only after payment, final artwork approval and structure confirmation.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> Can I order a physical prototype before a larger project?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>A physical prototype may be available for a qualifying bulk or wholesale project. Where approved, its cost may be adjusted into the resulting order. It is not an unrestricted free-prototype or free-sample program, and one-box production is not offered as a standard route. Share the project quantity, structure, print and prototype purpose during quoting so the team can confirm whether a physical sample is appropriate. Every approved order still receives a digital proof before production.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> How long does production take, and when does the timing begin?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>Production normally takes 4–6 business days after payment, final artwork approval and structure confirmation. Production-ready jobs are generally planned to print and ship within 7 business days. Shipping transit time is separate. Complex multi-design orders may need a separately confirmed schedule, especially where several short runs create additional setups. Provide complete artwork, quantities, design counts, destinations and timing requirements early so the production plan reflects the full order before approval.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                                    
                <div class="accord" data-expanded="false">
                    <button class="accord-title"> Can you handle multi-design orders or ship to several addresses?</button>
                    <div class="block custom_scroll">
                        <p>Yes. Multi-design programs can be planned, and one order can be shipped to multiple addresses. Workflows are also available for design firms and white-label projects. Provide the quantity for each design, destination details, packing instructions and target dates before approval. Several designs at low quantities can increase the number of production setups, so the schedule may need separate confirmation. Early planning keeps the quotation, production sequence and dispatch plan aligned with the real program.</p>                   
                    </div>
                </div> 
                            </div>
            <div class="showcase">
                                <img data-src="https://globalcustompackaging.com/theme/gallery_images/Faq-Section-Image-2.webp" src="data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2038%2038%22%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Faq Section Image 2" width="714" height="538" class="lazy">
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {

    const accordions = [...document.querySelectorAll('.accordions .accord')];

    // Cache each accordion's block element
    accordions.forEach(item => {
        item._block = item.querySelector('.block');
    });

    let activeAccordion = accordions[0] || null;

    // Open first accordion
    if (activeAccordion) {
        activeAccordion.classList.add('active');
        activeAccordion._block.classList.add('active');
    }

    accordions.forEach(item => {

        item.querySelector('.accord-title').addEventListener('click', () => {

            // If already open, close it
            if (activeAccordion === item) {
                item.classList.remove('active');
                item._block.classList.remove('active');
                activeAccordion = null;
                return;
            }

            // Close previously active accordion
            if (activeAccordion) {
                activeAccordion.classList.remove('active');
                activeAccordion._block.classList.remove('active');
            }

            // Open clicked accordion
            item.classList.add('active');
            item._block.classList.add('active');

            activeAccordion = item;

        });

    });

});
</script>

